=== Aggregation groups ===

The inputs with mode <tt>0xbc</tt> form the half-aggregation group of the transaction and the inputs with mode <tt>0xbd</tt> form the full-aggregation group.
The input with the highest index in a group is the final input of the group as defined in BIP 460.

=== Complete inputs ===

A half-aggregation input is ''complete'' when it carries <tt>PSBT_IN_CISA_HALFAGG_SIG</tt>.
A full-aggregation input is complete when it carries <tt>PSBT_IN_CISA_FULLAGG_PARTIAL_SIG</tt>.
An aggregation group is complete when all of its inputs are complete.

== Roles ==

=== Updater ===

The Updater sets <tt>PSBT_IN_CISA_MODE</tt> for every witness version 2 input that should take part in aggregation.
It can set the field to <tt>0x00</tt> to record that an input must not be aggregated.
It must not set or change the mode of an input that already carries a signature, a public nonce, or a partial signature.

=== Signer ===

The Signer reads <tt>PSBT_IN_CISA_MODE</tt> to determine how to sign a witness version 2 key path input.
A Signer must not create an opted-out signature for an aggregated input.

For a half-aggregation input, the Signer creates a BIP 340 signature over the half-aggregation signature message as described in the Signing section of BIP 460 and adds it in <tt>PSBT_IN_CISA_HALFAGG_SIG</tt>.

For a full-aggregation input, the Signer takes part in the signing protocol of BIP 459, with the PSBT taking the place of the coordinator.
If the input has no <tt>PSBT_IN_CISA_FULLAGG_PUB_NONCE</tt>, the Signer runs ''NonceGen'' and adds the public nonce in this field.
A full-aggregation input with a sighash type other than <tt>SIGHASH_DEFAULT</tt> must carry <tt>PSBT_IN_SIGHASH_TYPE</tt> before its public nonce is added.
Every signer computes the messages of all inputs of the group, and the partial signature does not encode the sighash type.
Once every input of the group has a public nonce, the Signer runs ''NonceAgg'' and ''Sign'' with the public nonces from the PSBT and the public keys and messages of the group as described in the Signing section of BIP 460.
It adds the partial signature in <tt>PSBT_IN_CISA_FULLAGG_PARTIAL_SIG</tt>.
'''Improper nonce usage can compromise private keys''', see BIP 459 for the requirements on nonce generation and usage.

An input whose output key is a MuSig2 aggregate key is signed with the workflow of [[bip-0373.mediawiki|BIP 373]] over the message of the input's mode.
For a half-aggregation input, the resulting BIP 340 signature is placed in <tt>PSBT_IN_CISA_HALFAGG_SIG</tt> instead of <tt>PSBT_IN_TAP_KEY_SIG</tt>.
Full-aggregation inputs with a MuSig2 aggregate output key are not covered by this document.

=== Combiner ===

A Combiner must fail when the PSBTs contain different aggregation modes, public nonces, or partial signatures for the same input, since such PSBTs do not belong to the same signing session.

=== Input Finalizer ===

An aggregated input is finalized together with the other inputs of its group, which requires the group to be complete.
To compute the messages and construct the witnesses, the Finalizer needs the witness UTXOs of all inputs and the sighash types of all inputs of the group.
The Finalizer first verifies every individual signature of the group, half-aggregation signatures with BIP 340 and partial signatures with ''PartialSigVerify'' of BIP 459, since the aggregation algorithms do not validate their inputs, see the Signing section of BIP 460.
It then computes the aggregate signature with ''Aggregate'' of BIP 458 or ''SigAgg'' of BIP 459 and constructs <tt>PSBT_IN_FINAL_SCRIPTWITNESS</tt> for every input of the group as specified in BIP 460.
An aggregated input that carries <tt>PSBT_IN_TAP_KEY_SIG</tt> must not be finalized, since that signature was created over the message of an opted-out input.

== Backward compatibility ==

These are new fields added to the existing PSBT format, which is designed to be extensible, so software without support for this BIP ignores them.
A Signer that supports witness version 2 key path spends but not this BIP ignores <tt>PSBT_IN_CISA_MODE</tt> and produces an opted-out signature, which the Finalizer rejects for an aggregated input.

== Rationale ==

=== Why no key data? ===

A witness version 2 key path input has exactly one signing key, the output key, so no key data is needed to attach the fields to a particular key, as for <tt>PSBT_IN_TAP_KEY_SIG</tt> in BIP 371.
Inputs whose output key is a MuSig2 aggregate key carry their participant data in the fields of BIP 373.

=== Why a separate field for half-aggregation signatures? ===

A half-aggregation signature is created over a different message than an opted-out signature and is invalid as a plain key path witness.
A separate field keeps a Finalizer without support for this BIP from building an invalid opted-out witness from it, and makes the mode of a signed input visible from its fields alone.

=== Why no field for the aggregate signature? ===

The aggregate signature belongs to the group rather than to an input, and both aggregation algorithms are public operations on the signature material in the PSBT.
The Finalizer therefore writes the aggregate signature straight into the final witnesses.

=== Why does the Finalizer aggregate? ===

Aggregation could also be a step of the Transaction Extractor, which is the only role with a transaction-wide view in BIP 174.
Aggregating in the Finalizer keeps the meaning of a finalized input as one that carries its final witness, so Combiners and Extractors without support for this BIP handle a fully finalized transaction as before.
It also matches BIP 373, where the partial signatures are aggregated before extraction as well.

=== Why an explicit opted-out value? ===

An absent mode field already means opted out, so <tt>0x00</tt> is not needed for signing.
It lets an Updater distinguish an input that must not be aggregated from one whose mode has not been decided yet.

=== Why are groups derived from the mode fields? ===

BIP 460 allows one group per scheme per transaction and orders each group by input index.
The group of an input and the final input of each group therefore follow from the mode fields alone, and a group identifier would carry no information.

== Test vectors ==

All inputs of the vectors spend witness version 2 outputs and carry the internal key in <tt>PSBT_IN_TAP_INTERNAL_KEY</tt>, and inputs signed with <tt>SIGHASH_ALL</tt> carry <tt>PSBT_IN_SIGHASH_TYPE</tt>.
[[bip_cisa_psbt/gen_test_vectors.py|gen_test_vectors.py]] generates the vectors, checks every extracted transaction against the key path rules of BIP 460, and writes them with the keys, messages, nonces, and signatures to [[bip_cisa_psbt/test-vectors.json|test-vectors.json]].

